# Release signing keys, in ssh-keygen's ALLOWED SIGNERS format (man ssh-keygen,
# "ALLOWED SIGNERS"), one key per line:
#   <principal> namespaces="git" <key type> <base64 public key>
# for example
#   releases@example.invalid namespaces="git" ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA...
#
# Scripts/install.sh publishes this file, root-owned, as
# /etc/blackbox/update-allowed-signers (publish_update_pins). While it holds no
# key, as shipped today, updates are not signature-checked and their log says
# so. Once it holds one, Scripts/update.sh and the in-app updater
# (Orchestrator/update/verify.py) install only commits SSH-signed by one of these
# keys: `git verify-commit` against this file alone.
#
# Signing releases (the owner, on the machine that holds the private key):
#   ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C blackbox-releases -f ~/.ssh/blackbox_release   # once
#   git config gpg.format ssh
#   git config user.signingkey ~/.ssh/blackbox_release
#   git config commit.gpgsign true          # every commit is signed from now on
#   git log --show-signature -1             # check before pushing
# The updater checks the commit it installs, which is the tip of main, so every
# commit that reaches main must be signed: merge and rebase locally and push. A
# merge made on GitHub's website carries GitHub's own signature, which is not in
# this file, and every box would refuse it.
#
# Adding the first key, or rotating one, is a commit to this file (sign it with
# a key the boxes already trust). A box enforces it from its next
# `sudo bash Scripts/install.sh`; Scripts/update.sh runs that on every update,
# the in-app updater never does. Keep a retiring key listed until every box has
# pinned its successor. docs/runbooks/2026-10-29-update-trust-and-service-user.md
# has the whole procedure.
